            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing tubs by price instead of cost per 25 g</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $20.99 Orgain tub looks cheaper than a $26.99 Meijer whey, but it holds about 210 g of protein against roughly 600 g. Always divide the price by total protein before deciding. The Meijer brand is about $1.12 per 25 g; the Orgain tub is about $2.50.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the small Gold Standard tub</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Optimum Nutrition's 1.47 lb tub at $35.99 works out to about $1.87 per 25 g, while the 3.89 lb tub at $79.59 is about $1.45 to $1.50. If you already know you like it, the bigger tub saves around 20% per gram. Use the small tub only to test a new flavor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the house brand is certified</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Meijer's whey shows no NSF, Informed Choice or similar certification that we could find. That is common for store brands and does not make it unsafe, but tested athletes should buy a product listed in a certifier's database, such as Gold Standard Double Rich Chocolate or Vanilla Ice Cream.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Paying delivery-app prices for a pantry staple</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Instacart and Uber Eats listings shopped from Meijer can show different, often higher, prices than Meijer.com or the shelf tag. For a tub you buy every month, Meijer pickup or an in-store trip with mPerks coupons is usually the cheaper route, and the cost-per-25 g sum makes the difference plain.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the serving size on the panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Meijer's whey needs 44 to 48 g of powder for 30 g of protein, while Gold Standard gets 24 g from 31 g. A bigger scoop means more carbs and calories from fillers. If you track calories or carbs closely, compare protein per gram of powder, not just protein per scoop.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
